## 2. NumPy

### Why NumPy over Python lists?
- Python lists need loops for operations
- NumPy operates on ALL elements simultaneously
- Handles millions of numbers efficiently

### Key Concepts

```python
import numpy as np

# 1D Array
scores = np.array([45, 23, 67, 12, 89, 34, 56, 78, 9, 43, 21])
print("All scores:", scores)
print("Scores + 10 bonus:", scores + 10)
print("Average:", np.mean(scores))
print("Highest:", np.max(scores))
print("Lowest:", np.min(scores))

# 2D Array — rows and columns
marks = np.array([
    [85, 90, 78],   # Student 1
    [67, 45, 89],   # Student 2
    [92, 88, 76]    # Student 3
])

print("Shape:", marks.shape)         # (3, 3)
print("Student 1 marks:", marks[0])  # First row
print("All Math marks:", marks[:, 0]) # First column

# Real world example — Swiggy customers
# [age, orders_per_month, avg_order_value]
customers = np.array([
    [25, 8,  350],
    [32, 3,  800],
    [19, 15, 150],
    [45, 2,  1200],
    [28, 10, 420]
])

print("Dataset shape:", customers.shape)       # (5, 3)
print("Number of customers:", customers.shape[0])
print("Number of features:", customers.shape[1])
print("Average age:", np.mean(customers[:, 0]))
```

### Key Rule
```
Rows    = individual data points (one customer, one image, one email)
Columns = features (age, price, pixel value)
```

