## 3. Pandas

### Core Structures
- **Series** — one column (single Excel column)
- **DataFrame** — full table (rows + columns like Excel sheet)

### Creating a DataFrame
```python
import pandas as pd

data = {
    'Name':   ['Rahul', 'Priya', 'Kiran', 'Ananya', 'Ravi'],
    'Age':    [25, 32, 19, 45, 28],
    'City':   ['Hyderabad', 'Mumbai', 'Bangalore', 'Chennai', 'Hyderabad'],
    'Orders': [8, 3, 15, 2, 10],
    'Spent':  [2400, 8000, 1500, 12000, 4200]
}
df = pd.DataFrame(data)
```

### Daily Morning Checklist — New Dataset
```python
df.head()           # First 5 rows
df.tail(3)          # Last 3 rows
df.shape            # (rows, columns)
df.columns          # Column names
df.dtypes           # Data types
df.describe()       # Stats — mean, min, max, std
df.isnull().sum()   # Missing values per column
df.info()           # Full summary
```

### Selecting Data
```python
# Single column → Series
df['Name']

# Multiple columns → DataFrame
df[['Name', 'City', 'Spent']]

# By position (iloc)
df.iloc[0]          # First row
df.iloc[0:3]        # First 3 rows
df.iloc[1, 3]       # Row 1, Column 3

# By label (loc)
df.loc[0]           # Row with index 0
df.loc[0, 'Name']   # Row 0, column Name
```

> **iloc = by position number | loc = by label name**

### Filtering Rows
```python
# Single condition
df[df['City'] == 'Hyderabad']
df[df['Age'] > 25]
df[df['Spent'] > 5000]

# Multiple conditions
df[(df['City'] == 'Hyderabad') & (df['Age'] > 20)]   # AND
df[(df['Spent'] > 5000) | (df['Orders'] > 10)]        # OR

# NOT condition
df[df['City'] != 'Mumbai']

# Filter + select columns
df[df['Age'] > 25][['Name', 'Spent']]
```

### Adding & Modifying Columns
```python
# Calculated column
df['Avg_per_order'] = df['Spent'] / df['Orders']

# Condition-based column (lambda)
df['Customer_type'] = df['Spent'].apply(
    lambda x: 'Premium' if x > 5000 else 'Regular'
)

# Note: variable inside lambda can be named anything
# lambda x, lambda val, lambda score — all identical
```

### GroupBy — Most Powerful Operation
```python
# Average spent per city
df.groupby('City')['Spent'].mean()

# Total orders per city
df.groupby('City')['Orders'].sum()

# Multiple aggregations
df.groupby('City').agg({
    'Spent':  'mean',
    'Orders': 'sum',
    'Name':   'count'
})

# Sort results
df.groupby('City')['Spent'].mean().sort_values(ascending=False)
```

> **Pattern:** `df.groupby('category')['value'].operation()`
> **Important:** groupby uses **parentheses** not square brackets!

### Handling Missing Values
```python
import numpy as np

data = {
    'Name':  ['Rahul', 'Priya', 'Kiran', 'Ananya', 'Ravi'],
    'Age':   [25, None, 19, 45, None],
    'Spent': [2400, 8000, None, 12000, 4200]
}
df2 = pd.DataFrame(data)

# Check missing values
df2.isnull().sum()

# Drop rows with missing values
df2.dropna()

# Fill with mean
df2['Age'] = df2['Age'].fillna(df2['Age'].mean())

# Fill with 0
df2['Spent'] = df2['Spent'].fillna(0)

# Always save back with = or use inplace=True
df2['Age'].fillna(df2['Age'].mean(), inplace=True)
```

| Situation | Use |
|---|---|
| Very few missing rows (<5%) | `dropna()` |
| Many missing rows | `fillna()` |
| Numerical columns | Fill with mean/median |
| Too many missing (>50% of column) | Drop the entire column |

