## 4. Matplotlib

### 3 Charts You'll Use 90% of the Time
```python
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# Bar chart — comparing categories
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 4))
plt.bar(df['Name'], df['Spent'], color='steelblue')
plt.title('Amount Spent per Customer')
plt.xlabel('Customer')
plt.ylabel('Amount Spent (₹)')
plt.show()

# Line chart — trends over time
months = ['Jan', 'Feb', 'Mar', 'Apr', 'May']
orders = [120, 135, 98, 167, 189]

plt.figure(figsize=(8, 4))
plt.plot(months, orders, marker='o', color='green')
plt.title('Orders per Month')
plt.xlabel('Month')
plt.ylabel('Orders')
plt.show()

# Histogram — distribution of values
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 4))
plt.hist(df['Age'], bins=5, color='orange')
plt.title('Age Distribution')
plt.xlabel('Age')
plt.ylabel('Count')
plt.show()
```

